<!-- [EN Sync v7.0] -->
<div class="lf-trap-card" data-trap="T1" data-hq-id="LF-T1-AI">
            <div class="lf-trap-header">
                <span class="lf-trap-badge">T1</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-diff">⭐⭐</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-id">LF-T1-AI</span>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="lf-trap-q">Which of the following figures is a symmetrical figure?\nA. Parallelogram\nB. Square\nC. Trapezoid\nD. Right triangle</div>
                <div class="lf-trap-wrong">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Common Mistake</span>
                    <span >Students choose A, because the diagonals of a parallelogram are equal, mistakenly thinking it has an axis of symmetry.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-right">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Correct Approach</span>
                    <span >The correct answer is B. A square has 4 axes of symmetry (two diagonals and two midlines) and is a symmetrical figure. A parallelogram has no axis of symmetry unless it is a rhombus or rectangle.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-note">💡 Remember: The two sides must completely overlap when folded along the axis of symmetry; equal diagonals do not mean symmetry.</div>
            </div>
        </div>
<div class="lf-trap-card" data-trap="T2" data-hq-id="LF-T2-AI">
            <div class="lf-trap-header">
                <span class="lf-trap-badge">T2</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-diff">⭐⭐⭐</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-id">LF-T2-AI</span>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="lf-trap-q">The figure below is half of a symmetrical figure, and the dotted line is the axis of symmetry. If there are 3 circles on the left, how many circles should there be on the right?\n(Diagram: 3 circles on the left, dotted line in the middle)</div>
                <div class="lf-trap-wrong">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Common Mistake</span>
                    <span >Students answer 3, thinking the numbers on both sides are the same, but they overlook that a circle on the axis of symmetry should be counted once.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-right">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Correct Approach</span>
                    <span >The correct answer is 3. In a symmetrical figure, the number of circles on the left and right sides is the same. There are 3 on the left, so there are also 3 on the right, making a total of 6. Note: If there is a circle on the axis of symmetry, it is counted only once.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-note">💡 The patterns on both sides of the axis of symmetry must be exactly the same, but a point on the axis belongs to only one side.</div>
            </div>
        </div>
